<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AgoraPackageName>Signaling</AgoraPackageName>
    <!--
      AgoraRtmKit and aosl are dynamic frameworks, so they cannot be embedded into this assembly
      and linked from there — the consuming app's linker never sees them. They travel beside the
      assembly instead; see the NativeReference block below.
    -->
    <NoBindingEmbedding>true</NoBindingEmbedding>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <VersionPrefix>$(AgoraSignalingIosVersion).$(AgoraSignalingBindingRevision)</VersionPrefix>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="../Agora.Binding.props" />

  <Target Name="ValidateAgoraXcframeworks" BeforeTargets="Build;Pack"
          Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/lib/AgoraRtmKit.xcframework')">
    <Error Text="lib/AgoraRtmKit.xcframework is missing. Run build/fetch-signaling.sh first." />
  </Target>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ObjcBindingApiDefinition Include="ApiDefinition.cs" />
    <ObjcBindingCoreSource Include="StructsAndEnums.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    How the frameworks reach the consuming app — same mechanics as the RTC bindings: dynamic
    frameworks packed beside the assembly as <name>.resources.zip, unpacked and linked by the
    Apple SDK in whichever app references this package.

    AgoraRtmKit links exactly one @rpath dependency, aosl (confirmed with `otool -L`). Fetched by
    build/fetch-signaling.sh from Agora's own SDK downloads, so they are not in git — including the
    visionOS slice stripping that script performs.

    This package coexists with either RTC package in one app — different framework names,
    different classes — but only because aosl is handled deliberately. Both products ship an
    aosl.xcframework, and both land at Frameworks/aosl.framework in the consuming app: the bundle
    carries a single copy, and which one it is, is whichever the build happened to keep. Up to
    2.2.6.2 they were not the same library. AgoraRtm_iOS bundles aosl 1.2.13, the RTC pods bundle
    1.3.5, and 1.2.13 does not export aosl_ref_magic — so an app that referenced both could end
    up unable to load AgoraRtcKit at all, with nothing about the build to say so.

    So the bundled copy is discarded and aosl comes from AgoraInfra_iOS (AgoraSignalingInfraIosVersion
    in Directory.Build.props), which at 1.3.5 carries the RTC pods' aosl byte for byte — the same
    pod Chat already takes aosl from, for the different reason that Chat bundles none at all.
    Verified in two places that fail rather than warn: the fetch script checks the staged framework
    exports aosl_ref_magic, and tests/Net.Agora.iOS.PackageTests/AoslCompatibilityTests.cs checks
    every packed aosl against every aosl symbol the SDK frameworks here import. Both directions
    were checked before the switch — all 89 aosl symbols AgoraRtmKit imports are exported by 1.3.5
    too, so nothing regressed for a consumer using Signaling on its own.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <NativeReference Include="lib/AgoraRtmKit.xcframework">
      <Kind>Framework</Kind>
    </NativeReference>
    <NativeReference Include="lib/aosl.xcframework">
      <Kind>Framework</Kind>
    </NativeReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>.NET for iOS / .NET MAUI bindings for the Agora Signaling (RTM) iOS SDK (AgoraRtm_iOS $(AgoraSignalingIosVersion)). Lets a .NET app log in to Agora Signaling, subscribe to channels and publish/receive messages. Hand-written against the real headers — see ApiDefinition.cs for why, and for the bound surface.</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);Signaling;RTM;messaging;realtime</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
